to adapt them to the shape of the vessel.—'''5.''' To remove the beard or fringe from, as from oysters.

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To grow a beard, or become bearded. [Rare].
{{smaller block|<poem>Nor laughing girl, nor ''bearding'' boy,
Nor full-pulsed manhood, lingering here,
Shall add, to life’s abounding joy,
The charmed repose to suffering dear.</poem>
{{right|''Whittier'', Summer by Lakeside.}}}}

'''bearded''' (bēr′ded), ''a.'' [< ME. ''berded''; < ''beard'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' Having a beard.
{{smaller block|<poem>Then a soldier,
Full of strange oaths, and ''bearded'' like the pard.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, ii. 7.}}
It is good to steal away from the society of ''bearded'' men, and even of gentler woman, and spend an hour or two with children.{{right|''Hawthorne'', Twice-Told Tales, I.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''her.'': (''a'') Same as ''barbed''<sup>1</sup>, 3. (''b'') Having a train like that of a comet or meteor (which see).—'''3.''' In ''entom.'': (''a'') Having a tuft of hairs on the clypeus, overhanging the mouth. (''b'') Covered on one side with short and thickly set hairs: said of antennæ.—{{smaller|'''Bearded argali.''' See ''argali''.—'''Bearded griffin.''' See ''griffin''.—'''Bearded tit, bearded titmouse,''' the ''Panurus biarmicus''.—'''Bearded vulture,''' the ''Gypaëtus barbatus''.}}

'''beard-grass''' (bērd′gräs), ''n.'' The common name of (''a'') some species of ''Polypogon'', especially ''P. Monspeliensis'' and ''P. littoralis'', from the densely bearded appearance of the close panicles; (''b'') some common species of ''Andropogon'', as ''A. nutans'', ''A. scoparius'', etc.—{{smaller|'''Woolly beard-grass,''' a name given to species of ''Erianthus''.—'''Naked beard-grass,''' a name of species of ''Gymnopogon''.}}

'''beardie''' (bēr′di), ''n.'' Same as ''beardy'', 2.

