'''bear-hound''' (bãr′hound), ''n.'' A hound for hunting or baiting the bear.
{{smaller block|Few years more and the Wolf-hounds shall fall suppressed, the ''Bear-hounds'', the Falconry.
{{right|''Carlyle'', French Rev., I. iii. 1.}}}}

'''bearing''' (bãr′ing), ''n.'' [< ME. ''bering, beryng;'' verbal n. of ''bear''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1†.''' Support, as of a principle or an action; maintenance; defense.
{{smaller block|I speak against the ''bearing'' of bloodshed: this ''bearing'' must be looked upon.
{{right|''Latimer'', 5th Serm. bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}}}
'''2.''' The act of enduring, especially of enduring patiently or without complaining; endurance.
{{smaller block|The two powers which constitute a wise woman are those of ''bearing'' and forbearing. {{right|''Epictetus'' (trans.).}}}}
'''3.''' The manner in which a person bears or comports himself; carriage; mien; behavior.
{{smaller block|A man of good repute, carriage, ''bearing'', and estimation.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., i. 1.}}

I had reason to dread a fair outside, to mistrust a popular ''bearing'', to shudder before distinction, grace, and courtesy. {{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Shirley, xxiv.}}}}
'''4.''' The mutual relation of the parts of a whole; mode of connection.
{{smaller block|<poem>But of this frame the ''bearings'' and the ties,
The strong connections, nice dependencies,
Gradations just, has thy pervading soul
Look’d through? ''Pope'', Essay on Man, i. 29.</poem>}}
